 of elements in \\( S \\) divisible by 2, 3, 5, or 7 using the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n   - Multiples of 2: \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{2} \\right\\rfloor = 140 \\)\n   - Multiples of 3: \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 93 \\)\n   - Multiples of 5: \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{5} \\right\\rfloor = 56 \\)\n   - Multiples of 7: \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{7} \\right\\rfloor = 40 \\)\n   - Subtract overlaps (multiples of pairwise products):\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{6} \\right\\rfloor = 46 \\)\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{10} \\right\\rfloor = 28 \\)\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{14} \\right\\rfloor = 20 \\)\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{15} \\right\\rfloor = 18 \\)\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{21} \\right\\rfloor \\approx 13 \\)\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{35} \\right\\rfloor = 8 \\)\n   - Add back overlaps of three primes:\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{30} \\right\\rfloor \\approx 9 \\)\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{42} \\right\\rfloor \\approx 6 \\)\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{70} \\right\\rfloor = 4 \\)\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{105} \\right\\rfloor \\approx 2 \\)\n   - Subtract overlaps of all four primes:\n     - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{210} \\right\\rfloor = 1 \\)\n\n3. **Total Calculation**:\n   - Sum of individual multiples: \\( 140 + 93 + 56 + 40 = 329 \\)\n   - Subtract pairwise overlaps: \\( 329 - 133 = 196 \\)\n   - Add back triple overlaps: \\( 196 + 21 = 217 \\)\n   - Subtract quadruple overlap: \\( 217 - 1 = 216 \\)\n\n4. **Conclusion**: The maximum size of a subset of \\( S \\) without 5 pairwise coprime numbers is 216. Therefore, the smallest \\( n \\) such that every \\( n \\)-element subset contains 5 pairwise coprime numbers is \\( 216 + 1 = 217 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{217}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet \\( S = \\{1, 2, \\dots, 280\\} \\). We want to find the smallest natural number \\( n \\) such that every subset of \\( S \\) with \\( n \\) elements contains at least 5 pairwise coprime numbers.\n\n1. Consider the sets:\n   \\[\n   A_i = \\left\\{i \\cdot k \\mid k = 1,2, \\cdots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{i}\\right\\rfloor \\right\\} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad i = 2, 3, 5, 7\n   \\]\n\n2. Easily, we can compute the sizes of these sets:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   |A_2| & = 140, \\\\\n   |A_3| & = 93, \\\\\n   |A_5| & = 56, \\\\\n   |A_7| & = 40.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n3. And the intersections:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   |A_2 \\cap A_3| & = 46, \\\\\n   |A_{10}| = |A_2 \\cap A_5| & = 28, \\\\\n   |A_{14}| = |A_2 \\cap A_7| & = 20, \\\\\n   |A_{15}| = |A_3 \\cap A_5| & = 18, \\\\\n   |A_{21}| = |A_3 \\cap A_7| & = 13, \\\\\n   |A_{35}| = |A_5 \\cap A_7| & = 8.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n4. Furthermore, higher-order intersections:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   |A_{30}| = |A_2 \\cap A_3 \\cap A_5| & = 9, \\\\\n   |A_{42}| = |A_2 \\cap A_3 \\cap A_7| & = 6, \\\\\n   |A_{70}| = |A_2 \\cap A_5 \\cap A_7| & = 4, \\\\\n   |A_{105}| = |A_3 \\cap A_5 \\cap A_7| & = 2, \\\\\n   |A_{210}| = |A_2 \\cap A_3 \\cap A_5 \\cap A_7| & = 1.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n5. Let \\( A = A_2 \\cup A_3 \\cup A_5 \\cup A_7 \\). Using the inclusion-exclusion principle, we have:\n   \\[\n   |A| = 216.\n   \\]\n\n6. Since \\( A \\) has 216 elements, if we select 5 elements, at least two must be in the same \\( A_i \\) thus not pairwise coprime. Hence, we need:\n   \\[\n   n \\geq 217.\n   \\]\n\nNow, we need to confirm that \\( n = 217 \\) is indeed sufficient:\n1. Suppose \\( T \\subset S \\) with \\( |T| = 217 \\).\n2. Let \\( M \\) be the set of elements in \\( S \\) consisting of all prime numbers and 1:\n   \\[\n   |M| = 60.\n   \\]\n\n3. Consider three cases:\n   - If \\( |T \\cap M| \\geq 5 \\), problem's condition is satisfied.\n   - If \\( |T \\cap M| = 4 \\), let the remaining numbers be primes, \\( p_1 \\leq 11, p_2 \\leq 13, p_3 \\leq 17, p_4 \\leq 19, p_5 \\leq 23 \\). Then:\n     \\[\n     \\{p_1^2, p_1 p_2, p_1 p_3, p_1 p_4, p_1 p_5,...,p_2p_5\\} \\subset S,\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     |T| = 217 \\Rightarrow |S - T| = 63 \\Rightarrow \\text{Combine elements ensuring at least one satisfies.}\n     \\]\n\nBy pigeonhole principle, at least one elemnt set meets the criteria.\n\nThus, the smallest \\( n \\) such that every subset of \\( S \\) with \\( n \\) elements contains 5 pairwise coprime numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{217}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that if in a convex hexagon each of the three diagonals connecting opposite vertices divides the area in half, then these diagonals intersect at a single point.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that if in a convex hexagon, each of the three diagonals connecting opposite vertices divides the area in half, then all these diagonals intersect at a single point. Hmm, let me start by understanding the problem better.\n\nFirst, let me recall what a convex hexagon is. A convex hexagon is a six-sided polygon where all interior angles are less than 180 degrees, and every line segment between two vertices stays inside the polygon. Now, the problem mentions three diagonals connecting opposite vertices. In a hexagon, opposite vertices would be those separated by two edges. For example, in a regular hexagon, each vertex has an opposite one three edges away. So, in a convex hexagon labeled consecutively as A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, A6, the opposite vertices would be A1 and A4, A2 and A5, A3 and A6. Therefore, the diagonals in question are A1A4, A2A5, and A3A6.\n\nThe problem states that each of these three diagonals divides the hexagon into two regions of equal area. And the conclusion is that these three diagonals must intersect at a single point. So, I need to show that under the given area conditions, these diagonals cannot be concurrent but must meet at a common point. Wait, no, concurr