ng the weights \\(1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34\\) will satisfy the given conditions:\n   \\[\n   \\{x + 1 \\leq y + z\\}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible weight of the heaviest coin is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{34}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Suppose \\( d \\) is the greatest common divisor of the positive integers \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), and \\( d^{\\prime} \\) is the greatest common divisor of the positive integers \\( a^{\\prime} \\) and \\( b^{\\prime} \\). Prove that the greatest common divisor of the numbers \\( a a^{\\prime}, a b^{\\prime}, b a^{\\prime}, b b^{\\prime} \\) is equal to \\( d d^{\\prime} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to prove that the greatest common divisor (gcd) of the four numbers \\( aa', ab', ba', bb' \\) is equal to \\( dd' \\), where \\( d = \\gcd(a, b) \\) and \\( d' = \\gcd(a', b') \\). Hmm, okay. Let me start by recalling some properties of gcd. Maybe I can express \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) in terms of \\( d \\), and similarly \\( a' \\) and \\( b' \\) in terms of \\( d' \\). That often helps with gcd problems.\n\nLet me write \\( a = d \\cdot m \\) and \\( b = d \\cdot n \\), where \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) are coprime integers because \\( d \\) is the gcd of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\). Similarly, let me write \\( a' = d' \\cdot m' \\) and \\( b' = d' \\cdot n' \\), where \\( m' \\) and \\( n' \\) are coprime as well. So now, substituting these into the four products:\n\n\\( aa' = d \\cdot m \\cdot d' \\cdot m' = dd' \\cdot mm' \\)\n\n\\( ab' = d \\cdot m \\cdot d' \\cdot n' = dd' \\cdot mn' \\)\n\n\\( ba' = d \\cdot n \\cdot d' \\cdot m' = dd' \\cdot nm' \\)\n\n\\( bb' = d \\cdot n \\cdot d' \\cdot n' = dd' \\cdot nn' \\)\n\nSo all four numbers are multiples of \\( dd' \\). Therefore, \\( dd' \\) is a common divisor of all four numbers. Now, the question is whether it's the greatest common divisor. To confirm that, I need to show that any commo