late the distance \\(OD_1\\):**\n   - Using the coordinates of \\(O\\) \\((\\sqrt{99}, a - \\sqrt{91}, a - \\sqrt{99})\\) and \\(D_1\\) \\((0, a, a)\\):\n     \\[\n     OD_1 = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{99} - 0)^2 + (a - \\sqrt{91} - a)^2 + (a - \\sqrt{99} - a)^2}\n     \\]\n     Simplifying the differences:\n     \\[\n     OD_1 = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{99})^2 + (-\\sqrt{91})^2 + (-\\sqrt{99})^2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = \\sqrt{99 + 91 + 99} = \\sqrt{289} = 17\n     \\]\n\nThus, the length of the segment \\(OD_1\\) is \\(\\boxed{17}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the problem, we need to find the length of the segment \\( O D_{1} \\). Here are the detailed steps:\n\n1. **Understanding the Intersection with the Faces**:\n    - The sphere intersects face \\( A A_{1} D_{1} D \\) with a circle of radius \\( r = 1 \\).\n    - The sphere intersects face \\( A_{1} B_{1} C_{1} D_{1} \\) with a circle of radius \\( r = 1 \\).\n    - The sphere intersects face \\( C D D_{1} C_{1} \\) with a circle of radius \\( r = 3 \\).\n\n2. **Calculate Distance from \\( O \\) to Plane \\( C D D_{1} C_{1} \\)**:\n    - Let \\( \\omega \\) be the circle of radius \\( r = 3 \\) that's on the face \\( C D D_{1} C_{1} \\).\n    - Drop a perpendicular \\( OX \\) from \\( O \\) to the plane of \\( \\omega \\). Point \\( X \\) will be the center of the circle \\( \\omega \\).\n    - Since \\( OX \\perp \\text{plane } C D D_{1} C_{1} \\), by the Pythagorean theorem in triangle \\( OXY \\) where \\( OY = 10 \\) (radius of the sphere) and \\( XY = 3 \\), we get:\n      \\[\n      OX^2 = OY^2 - XY^2 = 10^2 - 3^2 = 100 - 9 = 91\n      \\]\n      So,\n      \\[\n      OX = \\sqrt{91}\n      \\]\n\n3. **Calculate Distance from \\( O \\) to Plane \\( A_{1} B_{1} C_{1} D_{1} \\) and Plane \\( A D D_{1} A_{1} \\)**:\n    - Both planes similarly have circles of radius \\( r = 1 \\).\n    - Drop perpendiculars from \\( O \\) to these planes. Then the calculations for each plane follow similarly:\n    - By the Pythago