ach group is a set of parallel lines, and no two groups are parallel to each other, then the total number of intersections is the sum over all i < j of m_i * m_j. So, the problem reduces to finding a partition of 100 into integers m_1, m_2, ..., m_k such that the sum of m_i * m_j over all i < j is equal to 100 or 99.\n\nSo for part a), we need to check if there exists a partition of 100 into k \u2265 2 parts such that the sum of products m_i * m_j for i < j is 100.\n\nSimilarly for part b), check if such a partition can sum to 99.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using concurrent lines is another approach. If we have multiple lines passing through the same point, each new line through that point only adds intersections with lines not through that point. Let's say we have p lines passing through a single point. Then, the number of intersections contributed by this is: the p lines themselves don't intersect each other except at the common point. So, instead of C(p, 2) intersections, we have 1. So the reduction here is C(p, 2) - 1. Therefore, if we have several such concurrent points, we can subtract these reductions from the maximum 4950 to get the total intersections.\n\nBut since we need only 100 or 99, which is way less than 4950, we need a lot of overlaps either through parallelism or concurrency.\n\nBut maybe combining both methods.\n\nBut maybe starting with the parallel groups approach is simpler.\n\nLet\u2019s formalize the problem. Let\u2019s suppose we divide the 100 lines into k groups, each group with m_1, m_2, ..., m_k lines, all lines in a group are parallel, and groups are not parallel to each other. Then the total number of intersections is sum_{i < j} m_i * m_j. We need this sum to be equal to 100 (or 99 for part b).\n\nAlso, note that sum_{i=1 to k} m_i = 100.\n\nSo we need sum_{i < j} m_i m_j = 100.\n\nBut sum_{i < j} m_i m_j = [ (sum m_i)^2 - sum m_i^2 ] / 2. Because (sum m_i)^2 = sum m_i^2 + 2 sum_{i < j} m_i m_j.\n\nTherefore, sum_{i < j} m_i m_j = (100^2