g through a single point.\n2. Introduce the 100th line such that it intersects all of the previously drawn 99 lines.\n\nIn this configuration:\n- The 99 lines intersect at one common point.\n- The 100th line intersects each of the 99 lines at distinct points not on the common intersection.\n\nCounting the total intersection points:\n- The common point where all 99 lines intersect counts as 1 intersection.\n- The 100th line intersects each of the 99 lines in distinct points, contributing 99 additional points.\n\nThus, the total number of intersection points is:\n\\[ 1 + 99 = 100 \\]\n\nHence, the intersection points can indeed be 100.\n\n#### Case b): Can the number of all intersection points equal 99?\n\nConsider the following steps:\n\n1. Construct 99 parallel lines. Parallel lines do not intersect among themselves and thus contribute no intersection points.\n2. Introduce the 100th line such that it intersects each of the 99 parallel lines.\n\nIn this configuration:\n- The 99 parallel lines do not intersect with each other.\n- The 100th line intersects each of the 99 parallel lines exactly once.\n\nSo, the total number of intersection points in this setup is:\n\\[ 99 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of intersection points can indeed be 99.\n\nBoth results indicate that the given number of intersection points is possible in the aforementioned configurations. \n\n### Conclusion:\nFor the given problem:\n\na) Yes, the number of intersection points can be 100.\n\nb) Yes, the number of intersection points can be 99.\n\nThereby,\n\\[ \\boxed{\\text{a) Yes, it could be 100; b) Yes, it could be 99.}} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nLet the set \\( M = \\{1, 2, 3, \\cdots, 2002\\} \\). For any non-empty subset \\( A \\) of \\( M \\), define \\( x_{A} \\) as the sum of the maximum and minimum numbers in \\( A \\). Find the arithmetic mean of all such \\( x_{A} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have