k\u00b2 ) ] * sqrt( (r - h)^2 + k\u00b2 )\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\nBD = 2r(r - h) / sqrt( (r - h)^2 + k\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, now we can express AC \u00b7 AE and BD \u00b7 BE:\n\nAC \u00b7 AE = [2r(h + r) / sqrt((h + r)^2 + k\u00b2)] * [sqrt((h + r)^2 + k\u00b2)] = 2r(h + r)\n\nSimilarly, BD \u00b7 BE = [2r(r - h) / sqrt((r - h)^2 + k\u00b2)] * [sqrt((r - h)^2 + k\u00b2)] = 2r(r - h)\n\nTherefore, AC \u00b7 AE + BD \u00b7 BE = 2r(h + r) + 2r(r - h) = 2r(h + r + r - h) = 2r(2r) = 4r\u00b2\n\nSince the radius r is fixed (as AB is the diameter, so AB = 2r, so r = AB/2), the expression simplifies to 4r\u00b2 = (2r)\u00b2 = AB\u00b2. Hence, the expression AC \u00b7 AE + BD \u00b7 BE is equal to AB\u00b2, which is constant regardless of the position of E inside the circle.\n\nWow, that worked out! By parametrizing the coordinates and computing the distances, everything cancels out nicely, leaving a constant term dependent only on the diameter AB. So the key insight was to model the problem with coordinates and realize that after computing the distances, the variables h and k related to point E's position would cancel out, leaving an expression solely in terms of the diameter. Alternatively, noticing that when you compute AC \u00b7 AE and BD \u00b7 BE using the parametrization, each product simplifies to twice the radius times a term that, when summed, cancels the variable h, leading to a constant.\n\nBut maybe there's a more elegant geometric approach without coordinates? Let me think again.\n\nSince AB is the diameter, and C and D are points where AE and BE meet the circle again. We can use power of a point E with respect to the circle. The power of E is equal to EA \u00b7 EC = EB \u00b7 ED. Let's denote this common product as P = EA \u00b7 EC = EB \u00b7 ED. Then, AC \u00b7 AE = (AE + EC) \u00b7 AE = AE\u00b2 + AE \u00b7 EC = AE\u00b2 + P. Similarly, BD \u00b7 BE = (BE + ED) \u00b7 BE = BE\u00b2 + BE \u00b7 ED = BE\u00b2 + P. Therefore, AC \u00b7 AE + BD \u00b7 BE = A