ng Petya into a position where all outgoing edges have been flipped to incoming.\n\nHence, in a complex state, Vasya can always win by leveraging the cycles to create dead-ends through strategic edge flips.", "deepseek_solution": "**Solution:**\n\n### Part (a): Simple State (Tree)\n\nIn a simple state (a tree), Petya can ensure he never loses by employing the following strategy:\n\n1. **Initial Orientation:** Choose a non-leaf node as the root and direct all edges away from it. This creates an out-arborescence where each node (except leaves) has at least one outgoing edge.\n2. **Movement Strategy:** When the tourist is at a node with multiple outgoing edges, Petya alternates between them. Each time Vasya flips an edge, Petya uses another outgoing edge. If forced to a leaf, the edge can be flipped back, allowing the tourist to return to the parent node.\n3. **Cycling Through Edges:** By repeatedly moving between nodes with multiple edges, Petya ensures Vasya can't block all exits. The tree's acyclic nature prevents permanent cycles, but Petya's dynamic path selection avoids dead-ends.\n\n**Example:** In a star graph, Petya starts at the center, directing edges outward. Each move to a leaf allows a return after Vasya flips the edge, cycling through all leaves indefinitely.\n\n### Part (b): Complex State (Contains a Cycle)\n\nIn a complex state, Vasya can guarantee a win by:\n\n1. **Cycle Exploitation:** Focus on flipping edges within cycles. Each time Petya traverses a cycle edge, Vasya flips another edge in the cycle, disrupting its directed continuity.\n2. **Systematic Flipping:** By reversing edges strategically, Vasya creates conflicting directions, eventually closing all exits from a node. This forces Petya into positions where all outgoing edges are flipped to incoming.\n3. **Leveraging Multiple Cycles:** In graphs with multiple cycles, Vasya uses extra edges to trap Petya in a subgraph with no escape routes through iterative edge reversal.\n\n**Example:** In a triangle graph, Vasya flips edges opposite to Petya's path, reversing the cycle's direction. After three moves, all edges are reversed, leaving Petya with no valid moves.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \na) In trees, Petya's strategic edge orientation and dynamic path selection prevent Vasya from blocking all moves.  \nb) In cyclic graphs, Vasya exploits cycles to methodically eliminate Petya's escape routes, ensuring eventual victory.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nConsidering a graph where vertices represent cities and edges represent roads:\n\n#### Part (a): Proving Petya can always avoid defeat in a simple state\n\n1. **Graph Properties**: \n    - A simple state implies the graph is a tree (a connected acyclic graph). \n    - In a tree, there is exactly one path between any two vertices.\n\n2. **Petya's Initial Strategy**:\n    - Petya arbitrarily selects a root vertex.\n    - Direct all edges along the unique paths towards this root vertex, making a directed tree.\n    \n3. **Initial Move**:\n    - Petya then places the tourist on the root vertex.\n    - Since all edges are directed towards the root, any edge traversal will move the tourist towards the root under Petya's control.\n\n4. **Vasya's Responses**:\n    - Vasya can reverse the direction of one edge.\n    - However, as long as any edge leading out of the current vertex points towards the root, Petya can always move the tourist along one of these directed edges.\n\n    - **Cycle Avoidance**: If Vasya reverses an edge, Petya can always follow another edge leading towards the root. Thus, the tourist cannot be stuck unless Vasya reverses all outgoing edges from the current vertex, which is impossible in an acyclic graph.\n\n    Conclusion: In a tree, Petya can always move the tourist towards the root, ensuring he never loses. \n    \\[\n    \\boxed{\\text{Petya will not lose in a simple state.}}\n    \\]\n\n#### Part (b): Proving Vasya can guarantee a win in a complex state\n\n1. **Graph Properties**:\n    - A complex