eeded to pass the finite exceptions.\n\nWait, this part is a bit vague. Let me think again.\n\nIf there exists N such that for all n >=N, \\( a_{n+1} >=r a_n \\), then for any k consecutive terms that start at n >=N, the ratio is bounded by \\( \\frac{r}{r -1} \\). For terms that start before N, say start at n <N, but end at n +k -1 >=N. The number of such terms is finite (depending on k), but as k increases, these terms will eventually start at n >=N -k +1, which for large k can be negative. However, since the sequence is bi-infinite and increasing, the terms before N are bounded by \\( a_N \\). For large k, the sum of k consecutive terms starting before N would include terms from before and after N. The sum would be dominated by the terms after N, which grow exponentially. Therefore, the ratio for such sums would also be bounded by \\( \\frac{r}{r -1} \\). Therefore, overall, \\( b_k \\) would be bounded by a constant.\n\nCase 2: For every r >1 and N, there exists n >=N such that \\( a_{n+1} <r a_n \\). In this case, we can construct, for any k, a sequence of k consecutive terms where the ratio approaches k. Specifically, given any \u03b5 >0, there exists n such that \\( a_{n+1} < (1 + \u03b5) a_n \\), \\( a_{n+2} < (1 + \u03b5) a_{n+1} < (1 + \u03b5)^2 a_n \\), ..., up to \\( a_{n +k -1} < (1 + \u03b5)^{k -1} a_n \\). Then, the sum S of these k terms is < \\( a_n (1 + (1 + \u03b5) + (1 + \u03b5)^2 + \\ldots + (1 + \u03b5)^{k -1}) ) = a_n \\frac{(1 + \u03b5)^k -1}{\u03b5} \\). The maximum term is \\( a_{n +k -1} < (1 + \u03b5)^{k -1} a_n \\). Therefore, the ratio \\( S / a_{n +k -1} > \\frac{(1 + \u03b5)^k -1}{\u03b5 (1 + \u03b5)^{k -1}} \\). Simplify this:\n\n\\( \\frac{(1 + \u03b5)^k -1}{\u03b5 (1 + \u03b5)^{k -1}} = \\frac{(1 + \u03b5) - (1/(1 + \u03b5))^{k -1}}{\u03b5} \\).\n\nAs \u03b5 approaches 0, this expression approaches \\( \\frac{(1 + \u03b5) -1}{\u03b5} =1 \\). Wait, but we want the ratio to approach k. This seems contradictory. Maybe my approach is f