nt.\n\nHence, the sequence \\( b_k \\) either coincides with the natural numbers or becomes constant from some point onward.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the behavior of the sequence \\( b_k \\), we analyze the growth properties of the given bi-infinite increasing sequence \\( \\{a_n\\} \\).\n\n**Case 1: Sub-exponential Growth**\n\nIf the sequence \\( \\{a_n\\} \\) grows sub-exponentially (i.e., for any \\( r > 1 \\), there exist infinitely many \\( n \\) such that \\( a_{n+1} < r a_n \\)), then for any \\( k \\), we can find consecutive terms where each term is arbitrarily close to the previous one. This leads to the sum of \\( k \\) terms being approximately \\( k \\) times the largest term. Consequently, the supremum ratio \\( \\frac{\\text{sum}}{\\text{max}} \\) approaches \\( k \\), making \\( b_k = k \\).\n\n**Case 2: Exponential or Faster Growth**\n\nIf \\( \\{a_n\\} \\) grows exponentially or faster (i.e., there exists \\( r > 1 \\) and \\( N \\) such that \\( a_{n+1} \\geq r a_n \\) for all \\( n \\geq N \\)), the sum of any \\( k \\) consecutive terms is dominated by the largest term. The sum can be bounded by a geometric series, yielding a constant upper bound independent of \\( k \\). Thus, \\( b_k \\) becomes constant for all sufficiently large \\( k \\).\n\n**Conclusion**\n\n- If \\( \\{a_n\\} \\) grows sub-exponentially, \\( b_k = k \\) for all \\( k \\).\n- If \\( \\{a_n\\} \\) has exponential or faster growth, \\( b_k \\) becomes constant after some \\( k_0 \\).\n\nTherefore, the sequence \\( b_1, b_2, \\ldots \\) either coincides with the natural numbers or becomes constant from some point onward.\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{The sequence } b_k \\text{ is either the natural numbers or eventually constant.}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the problem statement, we are dealing with an infinite increasing sequence of positive numbers \n$$... < a_{-2} < a_{-1} < a_0 < a_1 < a_2 < ...$$\nthat extends infinitely in both directions. We are asked to show that the sequence \\(b_k\\), which denotes the smallest integer satisfying the condition that the ratio of the sum of any \\(k\\) consecutive members of the sequence to the largest of these \\(k\\) members does not exceed \\(b_k\\), either coincides with the natural numbers sequence \\(1, 2, 3, ...\\) or becomes constant from some point onward.\n\n1. **For \\( k = 1 \\):** \n   \\[\n   b_1 = 1\n   \\]\n   This is because the sum of one term divided by itself is always 1.\n\n2. **For \\( k > 1 \\):**\n   The problem introduces a condition involving the inequality for the sequence \\(b_k\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a_i + a_{i+1} + \\cdots + a_{i+k-1}}{\\max(a_i, a_{i+1}, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})} \\leq b_k\n   \\]\n   Given that the sequence \\(a_n\\) is strictly increasing, we can define \\(a_{i+k-1}\\) as the largest in any set of \\(k\\) consecutive terms. Hence, \n   \\[\n   \\frac{a_i + a_{i+1} + \\cdots + a_{i+k-1}}{a_{i+k-1}} \\leq b_k.\n   \\]\n   Since all terms are positive and increasing,\n   \\[\n   a_i + a_{i+1} + \\cdots + a_{i+k-1} < k \\cdot a_{i+k-1}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Bounding \\(b_k\\):**\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a_i + a_{i+1} + \\cdots + a_{i+k-1}}{a_{i+k-1}} < k\n   \\]\n   leading to:\n   \\[\n   b_k \\leq k\n   \\]\n\n4. **Case Analysis:**\n   - If the sequence \\( (b_k) \\) strictly follows the natural number sequence (i.e., \\(b_k = k\\) for all \\(k\\)), we are done.\n   - If at some \\(k\\), \\( b_k \\leq k - 1 \\), then for some \\( i \\), we have:\n     \\[\n     a_i + a_{i+1} + \\cdots + a_{i+k-1} \\leq (k - 1) \\cdot a_{i+k-1}\n     \\]\n     implying that:\n     \\[\n     a_i + a_{i+1} + \\cdots + a_{i+k-1} < (k - 1) \\cdot a_{i+k}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Stabilization of \\(b_k\\):**\n   Suppose the sequence \\(b_k\\) does not follow the natural number sequence. Then it must occur that:\n   \\[\n   b_k \\leq k-1\n   \\]\n   for some \\(k\\). Given that \\(a_n\\) is strictly increasing, and thus, this relationship holds for larger \\(k\\)