]{32 - 4} = \\sqrt[3]{28} \u2248 3.036\\)\n\nNot equal.\n\nx = -2:\n\nLeft side: \\(\\sqrt[5]{-8 -4} = \\sqrt[5]{-12} \u2248 -1.515\\)\n\nRight side: \\(\\sqrt[3]{-32 +4} = \\sqrt[3]{-28} \u2248 -3.036\\)\n\nNot equal.\n\nx = 0.5:\n\nLeft side: \\(\\sqrt[5]{(0.125) + 1} = \\sqrt[5]{1.125} \u2248 1.024\\)\n\nRight side: \\(\\sqrt[3]{(0.03125) - 1} = \\sqrt[3]{-0.96875} \u2248 -0.99\\)\n\nNot equal.\n\nx = -0.5:\n\nLeft side: \\(\\sqrt[5]{-0.125 -1} = \\sqrt[5]{-1.125} \u2248 -1.024\\)\n\nRight side: \\(\\sqrt[3]{-0.03125 +1} = \\sqrt[3]{0.96875} \u2248 0.99\\)\n\nNot equal.\n\nSo seems like only x=0, sqrt(2), -sqrt(2) are solutions. Let me check another value, say x=1.5:\n\nLeft side: \\(\\sqrt[5]{(3.375) + 3} = \\sqrt[5]{6.375} \u2248 1.475\\)\n\nRight side: \\(\\sqrt[3]{(7.59375) - 3} = \\sqrt[3]{4.59375} \u2248 1.668\\)\n\nNot equal.\n\nx=1. Let me check x= sqrt(2) \u2248 1.414:\n\nLeft side: sqrt[5]{(sqrt(2))^3 + 2*sqrt(2)}. As calculated earlier, equals sqrt(2).\n\nRight side: sqrt[3]{(sqrt(2))^5 - 2*sqrt(2)}. Also equals sqrt(2). So that works.\n\nSo maybe those are the only solutions.\n\nBut to confirm, let's see if the original equation could have more solutions. Let's consider the functions f(x) = (x^3 + 2x)^{1/5} and g(x) = (x^5 -2x)^{1/3}. Let's analyze their behavior.\n\nFirst, domain considerations. For f(x), since it's a fifth root, the radicand can be any real number because odd roots are defined for all real numbers. Similarly, for g(x), the cube root is defined for all real numbers. So the domain is all real numbers.\n\nNow, let's analyze the functions:\n\nFor f(x) = (x^3 + 2x)^{1/5}. Let's note that x^3 + 2x = x(x^2 + 2). So for x > 0, x^3 + 2x > 0, so f(x) is positive. For x < 0, x^3 + 2x = x(x^2 + 2). Since x^2 + 2 is always positive, the sign of x^3 + 2x is the same as x. So for x negative, x^3 + 2x is negative, so f(x) is negative.\n\nSimilarly, for g(x) = (x^5 - 2x)^{1/3}. Factor x: x(x^4 - 2). For x > 0, x^4 -2 can be positive or negative. For