nitude of g(x) grows faster. So similar to the positive side, after some point, g(x) is more negative than f(x). So they cross at x=-sqrt(2), and then diverge.\n\nTherefore, likely only three real solutions: x=0, x=sqrt(2), x=-sqrt(2).\n\nBut let me check another approach. Let's suppose that x \u22600, then perhaps divide both sides by x.\n\nBut original equation is:\n\n\\(\\sqrt[5]{x^{3} + 2x} = \\sqrt[3]{x^{5} - 2x}\\)\n\nIf x \u22600, factor x inside radicals:\n\nLeft side: \\(\\sqrt[5]{x(x^{2} + 2)}\\)\n\nRight side: \\(\\sqrt[3]{x(x^{4} - 2)}\\)\n\nSo:\n\n\\(\\sqrt[5]{x(x^{2} + 2)} = \\sqrt[3]{x(x^{4} - 2)}\\)\n\nIf x>0, then we can take x out of the radicals:\n\nLeft side: \\(x^{1/5} \\cdot (x^{2} + 2)^{1/5}\\)\n\nRight side: \\(x^{1/3} \\cdot (x^{4} - 2)^{1/3}\\)\n\nSet equal:\n\n\\(x^{1/5} \\cdot (x^{2} + 2)^{1/5} = x^{1/3} \\cdot (x^{4} - 2)^{1/3}\\)\n\nLet me take both sides to the 15th power to eliminate roots:\n\nLeft side: \\(x^{3} \\cdot (x^{2} + 2)^{3}\\)\n\nRight side: \\(x^{5} \\cdot (x^{4} - 2)^{5}\\)\n\nThus:\n\n\\(x^{3}(x^{2} + 2)^{3} = x^{5}(x^{4} - 2)^{5}\\)\n\nDivide both sides by x^3 (x\u22600):\n\n\\((x^{2} + 2)^{3} = x^{2}(x^{4} - 2)^{5}\\)\n\nHmm, this seems complicated. But maybe substitute t = x^2. Let t = x^2, so t >=0.\n\nThen equation becomes:\n\n(t + 2)^3 = t*(t^2 - 2)^5\n\nExpand both sides? This seems very messy, but let's see.\n\nLeft side: (t + 2)^3 = t^3 + 6t^2 + 12t + 8\n\nRight side: t*(t^2 - 2)^5. Let's compute (t^2 -2)^5 first.\n\nUsing binomial theorem:\n\n(t^2 -2)^5 = t^{10} - 10 t^8 + 40 t^6 - 80 t^4 + 80 t^2 -32\n\nMultiply by t:\n\nt^{11} -10 t^9 +40 t^7 -80 t^5 +80 t^3 -32 t\n\nSet equal to left side:\n\nt^3 +6 t^2 +12 t +8 = t^{11} -10 t^9 +40 t^7 -80 t^5 +80 t^3 -32 t\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\nt^{11} -10 t^9 +40 t^7 -80 t^5 +80 t^3 -32 t - t^3 -6 t^2 -12 t -8 =0\n\nSimplify:\n\nt^{11} -10 t^9 +40 t^7 -80 t^5 +79 t^3 -6 t^2 -44 t -8 =0\n\nSo we get an 11th-degree polynomial in t. Solving this seems impossible by hand. But we already know that t=0 (x=0) is a solution, but we divided by x^3 earlier, so t=0 is not considered here. Our previous solutions are x= sqrt(2), t=2, and x=-sqrt(2), t=2. Let's check t=2:\n\nPlug t=2 into the equation:\n\nLeft side: (2 +2)^3 =4^3=64\n\nRight side:2*(16 -2)^5=2*14^5=2*537824=1075648\n\nNot equal. Wait, that can't be. Wait, but we know that x= sqrt(2) is a solution. Wait, perhaps the substitution messed up.\n\nWait, no. Because when we set t=x^2, then the equation after substitution is:\n\n(t + 2)^3 = t*(t^2 - 2)^5\n\nBut if t=2, then left side: (2+2)^3=64\n\nRight side:2*(4 -2)^5=2*32=64. Oh! Wait, 2*(2)^5=2*32=64. Wait, but t^2 -2=4-2=2. So (t^2 -2)^5=32. Multiply by t=2: 64. So yes, t=2 is a solution.\n\nSo t=2 is a solution. Therefore, x= sqrt(t)=sqrt(2) or x=-sqrt(2).\n\nBut in the polynomial equation we derived:\n\nt^{11} -10 t^9 +40 t^7 -80 t^5 +79 t^3 -6 t^2 -44 t -8 =0\n\nIf t=2 is a solution, then substituting t=2:\n\n2^{11} -10*2^9 +40*2^7 -80*2^5 +79*2^3 -6*2^2 -44*2 -8\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n2^{11}=2048\n\n10*2^9=10*512=5120\n\n40*2^7=40*128=5120\n\n80*2^5=80*32=2560\n\n79*2^3=79*8=632\n\n6*2^2=6*4=24\n\n44*2=88\n\nSo:\n\n2048 -5120 +5120 -2560 +632 -24 -88 -8\n\nCompute step by step:\n\nStart with 2048:\n\n2048 -5120 = -3072\n\n-3072 +5120=2048\n\n2048 -2560= -512\n\n-512 +632=120\n\n120 -24=96\n\n96 -88=8\n\n8 -8=0\n\nTherefore, t=2 is indeed a root. So (t -2) is a factor. Let's perform polynomial division to factor out (t -2) from the polynomial.\n\nThe polynomial is:\n\nt^{11} -10 t^9 +40 t^7 -80 t^5 +79 t^3 -6 t^2 -44 t -8\n\nWe can use synthetic division for t=2.\n\nBut this might take time, but let me try.\n\nLet me write the coefficients for each power from t^11 down to t^0:\n\nt^11: 1\n\nt^10: 0 (since there's no t^10 term)\n\nt^9: -10\n\nt^8: 0\n\nt^7: 40\n\nt^6:0\n\nt^5: -80\n\nt^4:0\n\nt^3:79\n\nt^2: -6\n\nt^1: -44\n\nt^0: -8\n\nWe need to perform synthetic division with root t=2.\n\nSet up the coefficients:\n\n1 (t^11), 0 (t^10), -10 (t^9), 0 (t^8), 40 (t^7), 0 (t^6), -80 (t^5), 0 (t^4), 79 (t^3), -6 (t^2), -44 (t), -8 (constant)\n\nUsing synthetic division:\n\nBring down the leading coefficient: 1\n\nMultiply by 2: 2\n\nAdd to next coefficient: 0 + 2 = 2\n\nMultiply by 2:4\n\nAdd to next coefficient: -10 +4= -6\n\nMultiply by2: -12\n\nAdd to next:0 + (-12)= -12\n\nMultiply by2: -24\n\nAdd to next:40 + (-24)=16\n\nMultiply by2:32\n\nAdd to next:0 +32=32\n\nMultiply by2:64\n\nAdd to next:-80 +64= -16\n\nMultiply by2: -32\n\nAdd to next:0 + (-32)= -32\n\nMultiply by2: -64\n\nAdd to next:79 + (-64)=15\n\nMultiply by2:30\n\nAdd to next:-6 +30=24\n\nMultiply by2:48\n\nAdd to next:-44 +48=4\n\nMultiply by2:8\n\nAdd to next:-8 +8=0\n\nSo the result of the division is:\n\nt^{10} +2 t^9 -6 t^8 -12 t^7 +16 t^6 +32 t^5 -16 t^4 -32 t^3 +15 t^2 +24 t +4\n\nSo the polynomial factors as (t -2)(t^{10} +2 t^9 -6 t^8 -12 t^7 +16 t^6 +32 t^5 -16 t^4 -32 t^3 +15 t^2 +24 t +4)=0\n\nNow, we need to check if there are other real roots for the 10th-degree polynomial. This is difficult, but maybe t=2 is a multiple root?\n\nLet me try t=2 again in the new polynomial:\n\nCoefficients of the new polynomial:\n\n1 (t^10), 2 (t^9), -6 (t^8), -12 (t^7),16 (t^6),32 (t^5), -16 (t^4), -32 (t^3),15 (t^2),24 (t),4 (constant)\n\nUsing synthetic division with t=2:\n\nBring down 1\n\n1*2=2, add to 2:4\n\n4*2=8, add to -6:2\n\n2*2=4, add to -12: -8\n\n-8*2= -16, add to16:0\n\n0*2=0, add to32:32\n\n32*2=64, add to -16:48\n\n48*2=96, add to -32:64\n\n64*2=128, add to15:143\n\n143*2=286, add to24:310\n\n310*2=620, add to4:624\u22600\n\nSo t=2 is not a root again. Let's try t=1:\n\nEvaluate the polynomial at t=1:\n\n1 +2 -6 -12 +16 +32 -16 -32 +15 +24 +4\n\n= (1+2) + (-6-12) + (16+32) + (-16-32) + (15+24+4)\n\n=3 -18 +48 -48 +43= 3 -18= -15; -15 +48=33; 33-48=-15; -15+43=28\u22600\n\nNot a root.\n\nt= -1:\n\n1*(-1)^10 +2*(-1)^9 -6*(-1)^8 -12*(-1)^7 +16*(-1)^6 +32*(-1)^5 -16*(-1)^4 -32*(-1)^3 +15*(-1)^2 +24*(-1) +4\n\n=1 -2 -6 +12 +16 -32 -16 +32 +15 -24 +4\n\nCalculate:\n\n1-2= -1\n\n-1-6= -7\n\n-7+12=5\n\n5+16=21\n\n21-32= -11\n\n-11-16= -27\n\n-27+32=5\n\n5+15=20\n\n20-24= -4\n\n-4+4=0\n\nSo t=-1 is a root. Therefore, (t +1) is a factor. Let's perform synthetic division.\n\nCoefficients:1,2,-6,-12,16,32,-16,-32,15,24,4\n\nDivide by t=-1:\n\nBring down 1\n\n1*(-1)= -1, add to2:1\n\n1*(-1)= -1, add to -6: -7\n\n-7*(-1)=7, add to -12: -5\n\n-5*(-1)=5, add to16:21\n\n21*(-1)= -21, add to32:11\n\n11*(-1)= -11, add to -16: -27\n\n-27*(-1)=27, add to -32: -5\n\n-5*(-1)=5, add to15:20\n\n20*(-1)= -20, add to24:4\n\n4*(-1)= -4, add to4:0\n\nSo the result is:\n\nt^9 + t^8 -7 t^7 -5 t^6 +21 t^5 +11 t^4 -27 t^3 -5 t^2 +20 t +4\n\nThus, the polynomial factors as (t -2)(t +1)(t^9 +t^8 -7 t^7 -5 t^6 +21 t^5 +11 t^4 -27 t^3 -5 t^2 +20 t +4 )\n\nNow, the remaining polynomial is 9th degree. Let's check t=-1 again:\n\nEvaluate the new polynomial at t=-1:\n\n(-1)^9 + (-1)^8 -7*(-1)^7 -5*(-1)^6 +21*(-1)^5 +11*(-1)^4 -27*(-1)^3 -5*(-1)^2 +20*(-1) +4\n\n= -1 +1 -7*(-1) -5*1 +21*(-1) +11*1 -27*(-1) -5*1 +20*(-1) +4\n\n= -1 +1 +7 -5 -21 +11 +27 -5 -20 +4\n\nCalculate step by step:\n\nStart with -1 +1=0\n\n0 +7=7\n\n7-5=2\n\n2-21= -19\n\n-19+11= -8\n\n-8+27=19\n\n19-5=14\n\n14-20= -6\n\n-6+4= -2\u22600\n\nSo t=-1 is not a root again. Maybe t= -2?\n\nCheck t=-2:\n\nEvaluate the 9th-degree polynomial at t=-2:\n\n(-2)^9 + (-2)^8 -7*(-2)^7 -5*(-2)^6 +21*(-2)^5 +11*(-2)^4 -27*(-2)^3 -5*(-2)^2 +20*(-2) +4\n\n= -512 +256 -7*(-128) -5*64 +21*(-32) +11*16 -27*(-8) -5*4 +20*(-2) +4\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n-512 +256= -256\n\n-7*(-128)=896\n\n-5*64= -320\n\n21*(-32)= -672\n\n11*16=176\n\n-27*(-8)=216\n\n-5*4= -20\n\n20*(-2)= -40\n\nSo:\n\n-256 +896=640\n\n640 -320=320\n\n320 -672= -352\n\n-352 +176= -176\n\n-176 +216=40\n\n40 -20=20\n\n20 -40= -20\n\n-20 +4= -16\u22600\n\nNot a root.\n\nThis is getting too time-consuming. Given that we already have t=2 and t=-1 as roots, but t=-1 would correspond to x^2=-1, which is not real. So only t=2 is real positive root, giving x= sqrt(2), x=-sqrt(2). The other roots of the polynomial might be complex or negative, but since t=x^2 must be non-negative, only t=2 is valid. Therefore, the only real solutions are x=0, sqrt(2), -sqrt(2).\n\nTherefore, after thorough analysis, checking possible solutions, and considering the behavior of the functions, the solutions are x=0, x=sqrt(2), x=-sqrt(2).\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe real solutions are \\(\\boxed{0}\\), \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{2}}\\), and \\(\\boxed{-\\sqrt{2}}\\).", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the equation \\(\\sqrt[5]{x^{3} + 2x} = \\sqrt[3]{x^{5} - 2x}\\) in the set of real numbers, we start by eliminating the radicals. We raise both sides to the 15th power:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\sqrt[5]{x^{3} + 2x} \\right)^{15} = \\left( \\sqrt[3]{x^{5} - 2x} \\right)^{15}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n(x^{3} + 2x)^{3} = (x^{5} - 2x)^{5}\n\\]\n\nWe then test possible solutions by setting \\(x = y\\) where \\(y = \\sqrt[5]{x^{3} + 2x}\\) and \\(y = \\sqrt[3]{x^{5} - 2x}\\). This leads to the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^{5} = x^{3} + 2x\n\\]\n\nFactoring out \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx(x^{4} - x^{2} - 2) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives \\(x = 0\\) or solving the quartic equation \\(x^{4} - x^{2} - 2 = 0\\). Substituting \\(z = x^2\\), we solve:\n\n\\[\nz^{2} - z - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives \\(z = 2\\) (since \\(z = -1\\) is not valid), leading to \\(x = \\sqrt{2}\\) or \\(x = -\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\nWe verify the solutions:\n- \\(x = 0\\) satisfies the original equation.\n- \\(x = \\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(x = -\\sqrt{2}\\) both satisfy the original equation upon substitution.\n\nFurther analysis of the functions' behavior confirms no additional solutions. Thus, the real solutions are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}, \\boxed{\\sqrt{2}}, \\text{ and } \\boxed{-\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the equation:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[5]{x^{3}+2x} = \\sqrt[3]{x^{5}-2x}\n$$\n\nwe introduce two functions:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[5]{x^{3}+2x}, \\quad g(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x^{5}-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find $x_0$ such that $f(x_0) = g(x_0)$, let's denote the common value by $t_0$. Therefore, we can use the definitions of the functions:\n\n$$\nf(x_0) = t_0 \\implies t_0^5 = x_0^3 + 2x_0\n$$\n\n$$\ng(x_0) = t_0 \\implies t_0^3 = x_0^5 - 2x_0\n$$\n\nAdding these two equality expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\nx_0^3 + 2x_0 + x_0^5 - 2x_0 = t_0^5 + t_0^3\n$$\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n$$\nx_0^5 + x_0^3 = t_0^5 + t_0^3\n$$\n\nGiven that $x^5 + x^3$ is a strictly monotonic function over the whole real number set, the equality can hold only if:\n\n$$\nx_0 = t_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $x_0$ back into the original equations, we get:\n\n$$\nx_0^3 + 2x_0 = x_0^5\n$$\n\n$$\nx_0^5 - 2x_0 = x_0^3\n$$\n\nBoth simplify to the same polynomial equation:\n\n$$\nx_0^5 - x_0^3 - 2x_0 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring out $x_0$ we have:\n\n$$\nx_0 (x_0^4 - x_0^2 - 2) = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives us:\n\n$$\nx_0 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x_0^4 - x_0^2 - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nLet $y = x_0^2$. The quartic equation transforms into a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\ny^2 - y - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula, $y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$:\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 8}}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{1 \\pm 3}{2}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny = 2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = -1\n$$\n\nSince $y = x_0^2$ and $x_0^2$ must be non-negative, we discard $y = -1$. Hence, we are left with:\n\n$$\ny = 2 \\implies x_0^2 = 2 \\implies x_0 = \\sqrt{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x_0 = -\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are therefore:\n\n$$\nx_0 = 0, \\quad x_0 = \\sqrt{2}, \\quad x_0 = -\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nThese three values indeed satisfy the original equation, and there are no other real solutions.\n\nConclusion:\n\n$$\\boxed{x_0 = 0, \\sqrt{2}, -\\sqrt{2}}$$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "There is a system of two material points $\\boldsymbol{P}$ and $\\boldsymbol{Q}$ located on a certain ray. The masses of these points $p$ and $q$ and the distances $x$ and $y$ of each of these points from the beginning of the ray $S$ are known.\n\nWhat is the distance of the center of mass of the system from the beginning of the ray?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the center of mass of a system consisting of two material points, P and Q, located on a ray. The masses are given as p and q, and their distances from the start of the ray S are x and y respectively. Hmm, let me recall how the center of mass works for point masses.\n\nFirst, I remember that the center of mass (often abbreviated as COM) is like the average position of the mass in a system. For two points, it should be a weighted average based on their masses. But wait, since they're on a ray, which is a one-dimensional line starting from point S, the COM will also lie somewhere along this ray. The formula for the center of mass in one dimension should be straightforward.\n\nLet me think... For two masses m1 and m2 at positions x1 and x2 on a line, the center of mass position is given by (m1x1 + m2x2)/(m1 + m2). Yeah, that sounds right. So applying this formula here, with masses p and q at distances x and y from S, the COM distance from S should be (p*x + q*y)/(p + q).\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not mixing up any variables. The problem states that the masses are p and q, and their distances are x and y. So substituting into the formula, it's (p*x + q*y) divided by the total mass (p + q). That seems correct.\n\nBut let me double-check. Maybe I should derive it from scratch to confirm. The center of mass is defined such that the sum of the moments about that point equals zero. So, if we denote the center of mass distance from S as X, then the moment due to mass p should balance the moment due to mass q. But since they're both on the same ray, their moments are around S. Wait, actually, when calculating the center of mass, the moments are taken about the center of mass point. Hmm, maybe that's another way to approach it.\n\nAlternatively, the formula I remember is indeed the weighted average. Let me verify with an example. Suppose p = q, so both masses are equal. Then the center of mass should be exactly halfway between x and y. If x and y are the same, then the COM is at x (or y). If x and y are different, say x = 0 and y = d, with p = q, then the COM should be at d/2. Using the formula: (p*0 + q*d)/(p + q) = (0 + q*d)/(2q) = d/2. That checks out.\n\nAnother example: if p is much larger than q, the COM should be closer to x. Let's say p = 1000 kg, q = 1 kg, x = 0, y = 10 m. Then COM = (1000*0 + 1*10)/(1000 + 1) \u2248 10/1001 \u2248 0.01 m from S, which is very close to x = 0, as expected. That seems right.\n\nTherefore, the general formula should be (p*x + q*y)/(p + q). So the distance from the beginning of the ray S is (px + qy)/(p + q). Therefore, the answer should be this expression. But wait, the problem statement says \"the distances x and y of each of these points from the beginning of the ray S are known.\" So x and y are both measured from S, so they are positions along the ray.\n\nTherefore, the center of mass is calculated as the weighted average of their positions. Yes, that makes sense. So unless there's some trick or additional consideration here, this should be the answer. Let me think if there's any possible mistake here. For instance, if the points are on a ray, which is a half-line starting at S, but since they are both on the same ray, their positions are just scalar distances from S. So x and y are scalars, and the formula applies directly. There's no vector consideration here because it's one-dimensional. So yeah, the formula holds.\n\nAlternatively, if they were in different directions or in higher dimensions, we'd have to use vectors, but here it's straightforward. Therefore, the final answer should be (px + qy)/(p + q). Let me just check units to make sure. If mass p is in kilograms, x in meters, same with q and y, then the numerator is kg\u00b7m, denominator is kg, so overall unit is meters, which is correct for distance. All checks out.\n\nI think that's solid. The center of mass formula is pretty standard, and applying it here with the given variables gives the correct result. So unless there's a different interpretation of the problem, this should be right. The problem didn't specify any constraints on the positions or masses, so I think this is the general solution.", "deepseek_solution": "The center of mass of a system of two material points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) on a ray is determined by the weighted average of their positions relative to the beginning of the ray \\( S \\), with weights being their respective masses. \n\n**Formula Derivation:**\n1. **Masses and Positions:** Let \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) be the masses of points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\), located at distances \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) from \\( S \\).\n2. **Weighted Average:** The center of mass position \\( X \\) from \\( S \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   X = \\frac{p \\cdot x + q \\cdot y}{p + q}\n   \\]\n   This formula accounts for the contribution of each mass to the system's balance point.\n\n**Verification:**\n- If \\( p = q \\), \\( X = \\frac{x + y}{2} \\), the midpoint between \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n- If \\( p \\gg q \\), \\( X \\approx x \\), confirming the center of mass shifts toward the heavier mass.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{px + qy}{p + q}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the distance of the center of mass of the system of two material points $P$ and $Q$ from the origin of the ray, we can use the concept of the center of mass in a one-dimensional system. Here is the detailed step-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Identify Known Quantities and Their Locations:**\n\n   - Let $P$ and $Q$ be two material points with masses $p$ and $q$ respectively.\n   - The distances of points $P$ and $Q$ from the origin $S$ are $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n2. **Define the Center of Mass:**\n\n   - Let $Z$ be the center of mass of the system of the two material points, and let $SZ = z$ be the distance of the center of mass from the origin $S$.\n\n3. **Express the Ratio:**\n\n   - The ratio of distances from the center of mass to the two points $P$ and $Q$ is proportional to their masses. Thus, we can write:\n     \n     \\[\n     \\frac{PZ}{QZ} = \\frac{q}{p}\n     \\]\n     \n4. **Set up the Proportionality Relationship:**\n\n   - From the above proportionality, we can express:\n     \n     \\[\n     \\frac{z - x}{y - z} = \\frac{q}{p}\n     \\]\n     \n5. **Find the Center of Mass Using the Static Moment:**\n\n   - The center of mass can be found using the concept of the static moment. For the system of two material points, the static moment about the origin $S$ is given by:\n     \n     \\[\n     Mom_s(Z) = Mom_s(P) + Mom_s(Q)\n     \\]\n     \n   - In our system, this equates to:\n     \n     \\[\n     (p+q)z = px + qy\n     \\]\n     \n6. **Solve for $z$:**\n\n   - Rearrange the equation to solve for $z$:\n     \n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     (p+q)z &= px + qy \\\\\n     z &= \\frac{px + qy}{p + q}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n   \n### Conclusion:\n\nThe distance of the center of mass of the system from the origin of the ray is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{z = \\frac{px + qy}{p + q}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nCalculate the lengths of the arcs of the curves given by the parametric equations.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}\nx=\\left(t^{2}-2\\right) \\sin t+2 t \\cos t \\\\\ny=\\left(2-t^{2}\\right) \\cos t+2 t \\sin t\n\\end{array}\\right. \\\\\n& 0 \\leq t \\leq \\pi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the length of the arc defined by these parametric equations from t=0 to t=\u03c0. Let me start by recalling the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve. If a curve is given by x(t) and y(t), the length L from t=a to t=b is the integral from a to b of the square root of (dx/dt)^2 + (dy/dt)^2 dt. So, L = \u222b\u2090\u1d47 \u221a[(dx/dt)\u00b2 + (dy/dt)\u00b2] dt. Alright, so first things first, I need to find the derivatives dx/dt and dy/dt. Let's start with dx/dt.\n\nLooking at the equation for x: x = (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t + 2t cos t. To find dx/dt, I need to apply the product rule to each term. Let's break it down term by term.\n\nFirst term: (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t. The derivative of this will be the derivative of (t\u00b2 - 2) times sin t plus (t\u00b2 - 2) times the derivative of sin t. So that's 2t sin t + (t\u00b2 - 2) cos t.\n\nSecond term: 2t cos t. Similarly, the derivative here is 2 cos t + 2t (-sin t) = 2 cos t - 2t sin t.\n\nSo combining both terms for dx/dt:\n\ndx/dt = [2t sin t + (t\u00b2 - 2) cos t] + [2 cos t - 2t sin t]\n\nLet me simplify that. The 2t sin t and -2t sin t terms cancel each other out. Then we have (t\u00b2 - 2) cos t + 2 cos t. Combine those terms:\n\n(t\u00b2 - 2 + 2) cos t = t\u00b2 cos t.\n\nWait, that's nice. So dx/dt simplifies to t\u00b2 cos t. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nFirst term derivative: 2t sin t + (t\u00b2 - 2) cos t. Second term derivative: 2 cos t - 2t sin t. Adding them together:\n\n2t sin t - 2t sin t cancels. Then (t\u00b2 - 2) cos t + 2 cos t. Indeed, (t\u00b2 - 2 + 2) cos t = t\u00b2 cos t. Okay, that looks correct.\n\nNow let's do the same for dy/dt. The equation for y is (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t. Again, apply the product rule term by term.\n\nFirst term: (2 - t\u00b2) cos t. Derivative is (-2t) cos t + (2 - t\u00b2)(-sin t) = -2t cos t - (2 - t\u00b2) sin t.\n\nSecond term: 2t sin t. Derivative is 2 sin t + 2t cos t.\n\nSo combining these, dy/dt is:\n\n[-2t cos t - (2 - t\u00b2) sin t] + [2 sin t + 2t cos t]\n\nSimplify term by term. The -2t cos t and +2t cos t cancel each other. Then we have - (2 - t\u00b2) sin t + 2 sin t.\n\nFactor out sin t: [- (2 - t\u00b2) + 2] sin t = [ -2 + t\u00b2 + 2 ] sin t = t\u00b2 sin t.\n\nSo dy/dt simplifies to t\u00b2 sin t. Let me check again.\n\nFirst term derivative: -2t cos t - (2 - t\u00b2) sin t. Second term derivative: 2 sin t + 2t cos t. Adding them:\n\n-2t cos t + 2t cos t cancels. Then - (2 - t\u00b2) sin t + 2 sin t = (-2 + t\u00b2) sin t + 2 sin t = t\u00b2 sin t. Correct.\n\nSo, both derivatives dx/dt and dy/dt are t\u00b2 cos t and t\u00b2 sin t, respectively. Now, to find the integrand for the arc length, we need to compute \u221a[(dx/dt)^2 + (dy/dt)^2].\n\nCalculating (dx/dt)^2 + (dy/dt)^2:\n\n(t\u00b2 cos t)^2 + (t\u00b2 sin t)^2 = t\u2074 cos\u00b2 t + t\u2074 sin\u00b2 t = t\u2074 (cos\u00b2 t + sin\u00b2 t) = t\u2074 * 1 = t\u2074.\n\nTherefore, the integrand simplifies to \u221a(t\u2074) = t\u00b2. Since t is in [0, \u03c0], t is non-negative, so \u221a(t\u2074) = t\u00b2. That makes the integral much easier.\n\nTherefore, the arc length L is the integral from 0 to \u03c0 of t\u00b2 dt.\n\nNow, compute \u222b\u2080^\u03c0 t\u00b2 dt. The integral of t\u00b2 is (t\u00b3)/3. Evaluating from 0 to \u03c0:\n\n(\u03c0\u00b3)/3 - 0 = \u03c0\u00b3/3.\n\nWait, so the arc length is \u03c0\u00b3/3? That seems straightforward, but let me verify once more to ensure I didn't skip any steps or make any miscalculations.\n\nFirst, check derivatives:\n\ndx/dt: Correct, simplified to t\u00b2 cos t.\n\ndy/dt: Correct, simplified to t\u00b2 sin t.\n\nThen their squares sum to t\u2074 (cos\u00b2 + sin\u00b2) = t\u2074, so sqrt(t\u2074) = t\u00b2. Therefore, the integrand is t\u00b2, which is straightforward to integrate from 0 to \u03c0. The integral is indeed (\u03c0\u00b3)/3.\n\nBut wait, this seems surprisingly simple. Let me check if there's a chance that maybe the parametrization is a known curve where the arc length simplifies so nicely.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the parametrization is designed such that the speed (the integrand) simplifies. Given that the derivatives ended up being t\u00b2 cos t and t\u00b2 sin t, their squares add up to t\u2074, which is a perfect square, so the square root is t\u00b2. That's a nice property. So the curve's speed is t\u00b2, which when integrated from 0 to \u03c0 gives the total length as \u03c0\u00b3/3. \n\nAlternatively, could there be an error in computing the derivatives?\n\nLet me recheck dx/dt:\n\nx = (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t + 2t cos t.\n\nFirst term: d/dt [ (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t ] = 2t sin t + (t\u00b2 - 2) cos t.\n\nSecond term: d/dt [2t cos t] = 2 cos t - 2t sin t.\n\nAdding these: 2t sin t + (t\u00b2 - 2) cos t + 2 cos t - 2t sin t.\n\n2t sin t - 2t sin t cancels. (t\u00b2 - 2 + 2) cos t = t\u00b2 cos t. Correct.\n\nSimilarly for dy/dt:\n\ny = (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t.\n\nFirst term: d/dt [ (2 - t\u00b2) cos t ] = (-2t) cos t + (2 - t\u00b2)(-sin t) = -2t cos t - (2 - t\u00b2) sin t.\n\nSecond term: d/dt [2t sin t] = 2 sin t + 2t cos t.\n\nAdding these: -2t cos t - (2 - t\u00b2) sin t + 2 sin t + 2t cos t.\n\nAgain, -2t cos t + 2t cos t cancels. - (2 - t\u00b2) sin t + 2 sin t = (-2 + t\u00b2 + 2) sin t = t\u00b2 sin t. Correct.\n\nSo the derivatives are correct, leading to the integrand t\u00b2, and the integral from 0 to \u03c0 is indeed \u03c0\u00b3/3. Therefore, the arc length is \u03c0\u00b3 divided by 3.\n\nHmm. Let me think if there's another way this could be approached or if there's an alternative interpretation. For example, sometimes parametrizations can represent a known geometric shape, but given the equations here, it's a bit complex. However, since the derivatives simplified so nicely, perhaps the problem was constructed to have this property. Therefore, unless there's a miscalculation I missed, the answer should be \u03c0\u00b3/3.\n\nWait a second, but let me confirm with a different approach. Suppose I consider the parametric equations and try to see if they form a circle or some other shape. However, given the presence of t\u00b2 terms and combination of sine and cosine terms, it's not a standard circle. Alternatively, perhaps the curve is related to an involute or an Archimedean spiral, but given the parametric equations, let me see.\n\nWait, let's consider if x and y can be expressed in a simpler form. Let me see:\n\nx = (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t + 2t cos t.\n\ny = (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t.\n\nHmm. Let me see if there's a relationship between x and y. Maybe consider x and y as components of a vector or something. Alternatively, note that if we take the derivative of x and y, we get dx/dt = t\u00b2 cos t and dy/dt = t\u00b2 sin t. So, the velocity vector is (t\u00b2 cos t, t\u00b2 sin t), which is a vector of magnitude t\u00b2 pointing in the direction of angle t (since cos t and sin t are the direction components). Therefore, the curve is such that the velocity vector is t\u00b2 times the unit vector at angle t. That suggests that the curve might be related to integrating the velocity vector.\n\nIn other words, the position vector r(t) = (x(t), y(t)) has derivative r'(t) = t\u00b2 (cos t, sin t). Therefore, to get r(t), we need to integrate r'(t) from 0 to t. Let's see:\n\nIf r'(t) = t\u00b2 cos t i + t\u00b2 sin t j, then integrating from 0 to t would give:\n\nx(t) = \u222b\u2080\u1d57 u\u00b2 cos u du + x(0)\n\ny(t) = \u222b\u2080\u1d57 u\u00b2 sin u du + y(0)\n\nBut according to the given parametric equations, x(t) is (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t + 2t cos t. Let's check if integrating t\u00b2 cos t from 0 to t gives (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t + 2t cos t.\n\nLet me compute the integral \u222b u\u00b2 cos u du. Integration by parts. Let me set:\n\nLet\u2019s compute \u222b u\u00b2 cos u du.\n\nLet v = u\u00b2, dv = 2u du\n\ndw = cos u du, w = sin u\n\nIntegration by parts gives: u\u00b2 sin u - \u222b 2u sin u du\n\nNow compute \u222b 2u sin u du. Again, by parts:\n\nLet v = 2u, dv = 2 du\n\ndw = sin u du, w = -cos u\n\nSo: -2u cos u + \u222b 2 cos u du = -2u cos u + 2 sin u + C\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\n\u222b u\u00b2 cos u du = u\u00b2 sin u - [ -2u cos u + 2 sin u ] + C\n\n= u\u00b2 sin u + 2u cos u - 2 sin u + C\n\nTherefore, the integral from 0 to t is:\n\n[t\u00b2 sin t + 2t cos t - 2 sin t] - [0 + 0 - 0] (since at u=0, all terms are 0)\n\n= t\u00b2 sin t + 2t cos t - 2 sin t\n\n= (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t + 2t cos t\n\nWhich matches the given x(t). Similarly, compute \u222b u\u00b2 sin u du.\n\nLet\u2019s compute \u222b u\u00b2 sin u du.\n\nIntegration by parts:\n\nLet v = u\u00b2, dv = 2u du\n\ndw = sin u du, w = -cos u\n\nSo: -u\u00b2 cos u + \u222b 2u cos u du\n\nNow compute \u222b 2u cos u du. Again by parts:\n\nv = 2u, dv = 2 du\n\ndw = cos u du, w = sin u\n\nSo: 2u sin u - \u222b 2 sin u du = 2u sin u + 2 cos u + C\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\u222b u\u00b2 sin u du = -u\u00b2 cos u + 2u sin u + 2 cos u + C\n\nEvaluate from 0 to t:\n\n[-t\u00b2 cos t + 2t sin t + 2 cos t] - [ -0 + 0 + 2 cos 0 ] = -t\u00b2 cos t + 2t sin t + 2 cos t - 2\n\n= (-t\u00b2 + 2) cos t + 2t sin t - 2\n\nBut the given y(t) is (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t. Wait, the integral is (-t\u00b2 + 2) cos t + 2t sin t - 2. So that's (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t - 2. However, the original y(t) is (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t. So there's an extra -2 term. Hmm.\n\nBut this suggests that integrating r'(t) from 0 to t gives x(t) = \u222b\u2080\u1d57 u\u00b2 cos u du = (t\u00b2 - 2) sin t + 2t cos t and y(t) = \u222b\u2080\u1d57 u\u00b2 sin u du = (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t - 2. But according to the problem statement, y(t) is (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t. So there's a discrepancy here. The integral gives an extra -2 term. That suggests that either I made a mistake in the integration or the problem's parametric equations have a different constant of integration.\n\nWait, but when we integrate from 0 to t, the constant term is determined by the initial condition. Let's check the initial conditions at t=0.\n\nGiven x(0): plug t=0 into x(t) = (0 - 2) sin 0 + 0 * cos 0 = -2*0 + 0 = 0.\n\nSimilarly, y(0) = (2 - 0) cos 0 + 0 * sin 0 = 2*1 + 0 = 2.\n\nBut according to the integral for y(t), when we evaluate from 0 to t, we have y(t) = (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t - 2. At t=0, that gives (2 - 0) * 1 + 0 - 2 = 2 - 2 = 0. But the problem states y(0) = 2. Therefore, there must be an integration constant added. The integral of the derivative gives y(t) = \u222b\u2080\u1d57 dy/dt dt + y(0). But according to the problem, y(0) is 2. So the integral from 0 to t of dy/dt dt is y(t) - y(0). So, y(t) = \u222b\u2080\u1d57 dy/dt dt + y(0).\n\nTherefore, if \u222b\u2080\u1d57 dy/dt dt = (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t - 2, then adding y(0)=2 gives y(t) = (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t - 2 + 2 = (2 - t\u00b2) cos t + 2t sin t. Which matches the given equation. Therefore, the integral was correct, and the constants work out. So indeed, the parametric equations are the integrals of their derivatives plus the initial conditions. Therefore, all steps are correct.\n\nTherefore, since the integrand simplifies to t\u00b2, the arc length is \u222b\u2080^\u03c0 t\u00b2 dt = [t\u00b3/3]\u2080^\u03c0 = \u03c0\u00b3/3.\n\nTherefore, after thorough checking, the arc length is \u03c0\u00b3 divided by 3.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe length of the arc is \\boxed{\\dfrac{\\pi^3}{3}}.", "deepseek_solution": "To calculate the length of the arc defined by the given parametric equations from \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( t = \\pi \\), we start by recalling the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve. The length \\( L \\) is given by the integral of the square root of the sum of the squares of the derivatives of \\( x(t) \\) and \\( y(t) \\):\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFirst, we find the derivatives of \\( x(t) \\) and \\( y(t) \\):\n\n1. For \\( x(t) = (t^2 - 2) \\sin t + 2t \\cos t \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{dx}{dt} = 2t \\sin t + (t^2 - 2) \\cos t + 2 \\cos t - 2t \\sin t = t^2 \\cos t\n   \\]\n\n2. For \\( y(t) = (2 - t^2) \\cos t + 2t \\sin t \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{dy}{dt} = -2t \\cos t - (2 - t^2) \\sin t + 2 \\sin t + 2t \\cos t = t^2 \\sin t\n   \\]\n\nNext, we compute the integrand:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2 = (t^2 \\cos t)^2 + (t^2 \\sin t)^2 = t^4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) = t^4\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root gives:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{t^4} = t^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the arc length is:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} t^2 \\, dt = \\left[ \\frac{t^3}{3} \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = \\frac{\\pi^3}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe length of the arc is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{\\pi^3}{3}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo compute the length of the curve given by the parametric equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{l}\nx = \\left(t^{2}-2\\right) \\sin t + 2 t \\cos t \\\\\ny = \\left(2 - t^{2}\\right) \\cos t + 2 t \\sin t\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\] \n\nfor \\(0 \\leq t \\leq \\pi\\), we can use the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}} \\sqrt{\\left(x'(t)\\right)^{2} + \\left(y'(t)\\right)^{2}} \\, dt\n\\]\n\n1. **Find the first derivative of \\(x(t)\\)**:\n   \n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\left(t^2 - 2\\right) \\sin t + 2t \\cos t \\\\\nx'(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt} \\left[ \\left(t^2 - 2\\right) \\sin t + 2t \\cos t \\right] \\\\\n      &= \\frac{d}{dt} \\left[ \\left(t^2 - 2\\right) \\sin t \\right] + \\frac{d}{dt} [2t \\cos t] \\\\\n      &= \\left( \\frac{d}{dt} (t^2 - 2) \\sin t \\right) + \\left( (t^2 - 2) \\frac{d}{dt} (\\sin t) \\right) + \\left(2 \\frac{d}{dt} (t \\cos t)\\right) \\\\\n      &= 2t \\sin t + \\left(t^2 - 2\\right) \\cos t + 2 \\cos t - 2t \\sin t \\\\\n      &= t^2 \\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n2. **Find the first derivative of \\(y(t)\\)**:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\left(2 - t^2\\right) \\cos t + 2t \\sin t \\\\\ny'(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt} \\left[ \\left(2 - t^2\\right) \\cos t + 2t \\sin t \\right] \\\\\n      &= \\frac{d}{dt} \\left[ \\left(2 - t^2\\right) \\cos t 