 to show equality holds only if all degrees are equal, enforcing regularity. Thus, \\( k = 6 \\), and \\( m = 3n \\) remains valid.\n\n**Conclusion**: The number of pairs of acquaintances \\( m \\) must be a multiple of 3. Hence, \\( m \\) is divisible by 3.\n\n\\(\\boxed{3}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let's denote by \\( P \\) the number of pairs of acquaintances (this is equivalent to the number of edges in the corresponding graph).\n2. We also denote by \\( T \\) the number of triangles in this graph (where a triangle in graph theory is a set of three vertices each pair of which is connected by an edge).\n\n3. According to the problem's conditions, each edge belongs to exactly 5 triangles. Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   5P = 3T\n   \\]\n   This equation comes from the fact that every edge is counted in 5 triangles, and each triangle has 3 edges, leading to each edge being counted in 3 triangles once for each of its endpoints in the triangle.\n\n4. Since 3 and 5 are coprime (i.e., the greatest common divisor of 3 and 5 is 1), this implies that \\( P \\) must be divisible by 3. To see why, we divide both sides of the equation \\( 5P = 3T \\) by 3:\n    \\[\n    P = \\frac{3T}{5}\n    \\]\n   For \\( P \\) to be an integer, the numerator \\( 3T \\) must be divisible by 5. Since \\( T \\) is an integer, it follows that \\( T \\) itself must be divisible by 5. This implies \\( T = 5k \\) for some integer \\( k \\). Substituting \\( T = 5k \\) back into our equation, we get:\n    \\[\n    P = \\frac{3(5k)}{5} = 3k\n    \\]\n    Hence, \\( P \\) is indeed divisible by 3.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P \\text{ is divisible by 3.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\), if the sides \\( a, b, c \\) opposite to the angles \\( A, B, C \\) respectively form a geometric progression, then the range of the expression \\( \\frac{\\sin A \\cot C + \\cos A}{