refore, each pair of opposite faces (front-back, left-right, top-bottom) form a set where each member is connected to the four faces in the other two pairs. Therefore, the adjacency graph is indeed K_{3,3}. Yes, because if we take the three pairs as partitions, each node is connected to all nodes in the other two partitions. For example, front (in partition 1: front-back) is connected to left, right (partition 2: left-right), top, bottom (partition 3: top-bottom). Similarly, each node is connected to all nodes in the other partitions. So yes, the adjacency graph is K_{3,3}.\n\nTherefore, the cube's face adjacency graph is the complete bipartite graph K_{3,3}, which is a 4-regular graph with 6 nodes.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to labeling the nodes of K_{3,3} with distinct integers such that adjacent nodes differ by at least 2, and the total sum is minimized.\n\nTo find the minimal total, we need to assign the smallest possible numbers, spaced appropriately. Let's think about how to do this.\n\nSince K_{3,3} is bipartite, we can divide the graph into two partitions of three nodes each, where each node in one partition is connected to all nodes in the other partition. In our case, the partitions are the three pairs of opposite faces. For example, partition A: front, left, top; partition B: back, right, bottom. Each node in A is connected to all nodes in B except its opposite. Wait, no. Wait, in K_{3,3}, each node in partition A is connected to all nodes in partition B. But in our cube adjacency, each node is connected to four others (all except its opposite). So maybe the partitions are different. Wait, maybe the two partitions are the three pairs of opposite faces. Let me clarify.\n\nIf we take the three opposite pairs as the partitions, then each node in one partition (say front) is connected to all nodes in the other two partitions (left, right, top, bottom). Similarly, each node in the other partitions is connected to all nodes in the other partitions. So p