mploy Ceva's Theorem and properties of incenters.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Given Condition:**\n   \\[\n   \\angle APB - \\angle ACB = \\angle APC - \\angle ABC\n   \\]\n   Let \\( \\angle APB = x \\), \\( \\angle ACB = \\gamma \\), \\( \\angle APC = y \\), and \\( \\angle ABC = \\beta \\). This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   x - \\gamma = y - \\beta \\implies x - y = \\gamma - \\beta\n   \\]\n\n2. **Angle Relationships:**\n   - In \\( \\triangle ABC \\), \\( \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = 180^\\circ \\).\n   - At point \\( P \\), \\( \\angle APB + \\angle BPC + \\angle APC = 360^\\circ \\). Substituting \\( x + y + z = 360^\\circ \\), where \\( z = \\angle BPC \\).\n\n3. **Incenter Properties:**\n   - \\( D \\) is the incenter of \\( \\triangle APB \\), so \\( BD \\) bisects \\( \\angle PBA \\).\n   - \\( E \\) is the incenter of \\( \\triangle APC \\), so \\( CE \\) bisects \\( \\angle PCA \\).\n\n4. **Applying Ceva's Theorem:**\n   For concurrency of \\( AP \\), \\( BD \\), and \\( CE \\) in \\( \\triangle ABC \\), Ceva's condition must hold:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin \\angle BAD}{\\sin \\angle CAD} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin \\angle CBE}{\\sin \\angle ABE} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin \\angle ACF}{\\sin \\angle BCF} = 1\n   \\]\n   Here, focus on the ratios induced by the angle bisectors \\( BD \\) and \\( CE \\).\n\n5. **Angle Bisector Ratios:**\n   - In \\( \\triangle APB \\), \\( BD \\) bisects \\( \\angle PBA \\). By the Angle Bisector Theorem:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{AD}{DP} = \\frac{AB}{BP}\n     \\]\n   - In \\( \\triangle APC \\), \\( CE \\) bisects \\( \\angle PCA \\). Similarly:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{AE}{EP} = \\frac{AC}{CP}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Key Ratio Equality:**\n   Using the given angle condition and the Law of Sines in \\( \\triangle APB \\) and \\( \\triangle APC \\), derive:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AB}{BP} = \\frac{AC}{CP}\n   \\]\n   This ensures that the ratios \\( \\frac{AD}{DP} \\) and \\( \\frac{AE}{EP} \\) are equal, implying \\( AP \\), \\( BD \\), and