th the given property is if it's infinite.\n\nAlternatively, another angle: Assume S is finite, then consider the vector space generated by S over the real numbers. Since S is finite, the dimension of this vector space is at most 2. Let\u2019s say the affine hull of S is the entire plane, or a line, or a point. If it's a point, trivial. If it's a line or the plane, consider linear algebra.\n\nSuppose the affine hull is a line. Let\u2019s parametrize the points on the line with real numbers. Let\u2019s assign coordinates such that the line is the real number line. Then each point is a real number, and the midpoint condition becomes m = (a + b)/2 for each m in S. So, in this case, the problem reduces to showing that a set of real numbers where each number is the average of two others in the set must be infinite.\n\nSimilarly, in one dimension, suppose S is a finite set of real numbers where each number is the average of two others. Then, take the maximum number in S. Let\u2019s call it M. Since M is the average of two numbers in S, those two numbers must also be equal to M, because otherwise their average would be less than M. Hence, M = (M + M)/2, which is true, but this means that the maximum element can only be the average of itself and itself. If we require that each element is the average of two distinct elements, then the maximum element would need to be the average of two elements strictly less than it, which is impossible. Hence, such a finite set cannot exist. Therefore, in one dimension, any non-trivial set (with distinct elements) must be infinite.\n\nExtending this to two dimensions: If we have a finite set in the plane where each point is the midpoint of two others, consider the projections onto some line. The projection would satisfy that each projected point is the midpoint of the projections of two other points. If the projection is non-degenerate (i.e., not all points project to the same point), then by the one-dimensional result, the projection must 